DDA Strategy 17
Create Functional, Energy-Related Public Art
Project Leader:     DDA
Logical Partners:  Arts Cheyenne, City, State,
Educational, Nonprofit and Private Sectors

Need
Boots Walker, Cheyenne’s legendary cowboy singer/songwriter, helps
advance history while also promoting western-themed public art.  More
boots, please!  Mr. Walker’s partnership should be expanded from the
Cheyenne Depot Museum Foundation and DDA to include Arts
Cheyenne, Visit Cheyenne and private businesses.  Another theme for
public art was advanced in the 2006 Market-Based Downtown Plan by
P.U.M.A. – sculpture in the urban landscape that responds to
Cheyenne’s almost-constant wind.
Energy generated by wind and sun in urban areas is already used to
power street lights, phones and computers.  There are energy-gathering
façades and roofs in both new and rehab buildings.  Small wind turbines
rotate only three times per minute.  Think about the aesthetics of wind
power… functional kinetic sculptures, lights that mirror the wind’s
intensity, colors that change according to temperature, programmable
displays for holidays and every day.  Wind art — energy-generating
kinetic sculpture — can also become another tourism attraction, not in
the countryside but downtown.
Remedy
The strong prairie wind (that Boots Walker notes in his first song) can be
an asset for downtown,  A Request-For-Proposals (RFP) issued jointly by
DDA and Arts Cheyenne could attract well-known firms, environmental
think tanks and artists whose themes encompasses wind and solar
power yielding renewable energy.  The RFP could require entrants to
identify partnerships among businesses, researchers, artists, etc., akin to
the National Center for Atm ospheric Research (NCAR) joint venture
with the State, University of Wyoming, LEADS, Wyoming Business
Council, CLF&P and others.
Notes
In 1886, William Stanley produced enough power to supply one of the
nation’s first electric street light systems in Great Barrington,
Massachusetts.  Many thought Stanley was a crank.  Cheyenne could be
on to something big.
Specific Tasks
Responsible
Parties
Time
Allowance
Success
Metric
Budget
Allowance
1  Compile existing documents on Cheyenne’s wind and
solar energy potential.  Focus on the downtown.
2  Write a RFP for multi-disciplined responses to wind
and solar energy.  Make sure that artists are included.
Award bonus points for Wyoming-based submissions.
3  Jury the entrants; select winners.
4  Form an implementation partnership and attract
private sector investors.
5  Employ functional art.  Monitor energy savings.
6  License the art/energy producing product for sale to
others.

