### Page 46 of 84

```text
DDA Strategy 18
Launch Shared Food Truck Operations
Project Leader:     DDA
Logical Partners:  City County Health, Restaurant
Owners, Vendors, Entrepreneurs

Need
Anyone who has eaten on Depot Plaza during Cheyenne Frontier Days,
Fridays on the Plaza (and other events) knows about food trucks.
Catering trucks, BBQ trailers, taco-mobiles or mobile kitchens, are the
humble hot dog cart’s tony descendents.  In the last decade, they have
become a rage in urban settings.  They depend on foot traffic and are
run by start-up entrepreneurs (many of whom eventually open a
restaurant or food store).  Most offer good food at reasonable prices.  In
downtown Cheyenne, the concentration of  potential lunch-time foot
traffic is just a little far from most of the restaurants.  Even though it’s
just a seven-block walk from the Capitol complex or CRMC to 17th Street,
few can fit a quick lunch and a 14 block round-trip trek into one hour –
especially in the winter.  Why not take some of 17th Street’s tasty
affordable food to the employees just a bit north?
Remedy
DDA should consider launching a shared food truck operation in
downtown Cheyenne.  Interested restauranteurs, vendors and
entrepreneurs could bid on leasing the truck – first-come, first-served.
Terms for bids and leases are up for discussion, but a 24-hour period
would enable a vendor to offer breakfast, snacks and lunch; take-out
dinners are a possibility too.  Thus, at least five different cuisines – e.g.,
pizza or pasta and salads, Chinese, burgers, Thai, Tex-Mex (along with
soups and smoothies) – might be available each week.  Weekend leases
would be encouraged for pancakes, ribs, BBQ, chicken, etc.  DDA and
the vendors must:
• Agree on lease terms, e.g.,  times of operation; clean-up and
insurance; inspections by health officials, etc.;
• Plan sites for food truck operations complete with power and
running water, recepticals for litter, etc.
• Comply with federal, State and local regulations.
Notes
Experience shows that food trucks help build new customer bases for
downtown’s eat-in restaurants… a great way to engage locals in
Cheyenne’s growing “foodie” economy.
Specific Tasks
Responsible
Parties
Time
Allowance
Success
Metric
Budget
Allowance
1  Seek permissions/requirements/permits from the City
County Health Department.
2  Search the web for used food trucks.
3  Scout possible privately-owned locations for foot
traffic, power and running water.
4  Prepare an illustrated Questions & Answers (Q&As)
and Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) on food truck
operations (borrowing heavily from rules and regulations
from other cities).
5  Call open meetings with restaurant owners, vendors,
entrepreneurs, etc.
6  Discuss the broad picture… details of operation, terms
of operation, etc.
7  Solicit proposals; determine levels of interest.
8  Acquire the truck.
9  Monitor and report the results.

