```text
DDA Strategy 20
Enlist Downtown Stakeholders to Recruit Business
Project Leader:     DDA
Logical Partners:  All Those Interested in
Downtown Revitalization

Need
Placemaking (and other recent plans) produced an impressive list of
what people want downtown:  specific retailers, restaurants, services,
etc.  from next door (e.g., Dell Range) and faraway (e.g., Monrovia,
California).  This keen interest should be encouraged and harnessed to
help fill downtown’s empty spaces.  Attracting more merchants and
many more office workers could be assisted by a volunteer cadre of
downtown’s champions.
Remedy
In a series of facilitated sessions, stakeholders could be trained by local
economic development leaders – the Chamber, Visit Cheyenne, LEADS,
etc. – to prioritize, polish and perfect their wish-lists.  The volunteers
will be paid with full-value, 20 percent (or higher) Boots Bucks and/or
Positively Cheyenne coupons good at downtown merchants.  Among
other tasks, stakeholders could:
• Interview existing downtown merchants with professionally-
prepared templates.  Compare notes with other volunteers.
Compile the results;
• Help assemble an on-going inventory of all available downtown
space (Strategy 19).  How many square feet?  Where?  What’s the
physical condition?  Who’s the broker?  Who’s the owner?
• Start a public “I wish this was…” sticker campaign on vacant
storefront windows and compile the results;
• Research each target:  history; headquarters; near-by stores or
offices; population thresholds; Dun & Bradstreet rating, etc.  Edit
and test the recruiting case statement;
• Advocate for City, County and Wyoming incentives to attract
merchants and office tenants;
• Conduct welcome wagon tours for prospective businesses and their
employees.
Notes
Turn the wish-lists into reality.  Home-grown activism has worked
elsewhere and can yield good results for downtown Cheyenne.
