### Page 51 of 84

```text
City Strategy 22
Click and Complain:  Enhance the City’s “Request
Partner” System
Project Leader:     City
Logical Partners:  DDA
Need
Downtown Cheyenne’s
stakeholders routinely notice
(mostly) minor maintenance issues
requiring attention by the City or
other public agency partners –
from potholes and bent signs to
cracked sidewalks and curbs.
Every day that passes without
visible progress making repairs
increases frustration.  It also
diminishes faith in the public
sector and enjoyment of
downtown.
Some observers contact responsible parties by phone or by using the
City’s “Request Partner” system.  But too few are aware of this system;
without a mechanism for direct feedback, voicing concerns can feel like
shouting into the wind.  Many communities have created a suite of tools
to facilitate the process whereby citizens report a problem, receive
acknowledgement and an indication of when remedial action will occur.
This is followed by a final contact when work is complete.
Many cities rely on low- or no-cost Internet solutions provided by
SeeClickFix.com or Open311.org which offers ways to help citizens help
their communities.  However, Cheyenne also needs a pro-active
approach to supplement ad hoc citizen reports.  Ideally, downtown
Cheyenne’s stewards would be encouraged to notice and report such
matters via appropriate channels.
Remedy
This strategy encompasses three actions:
•
Encourage use of the existing web-based “Request Partner” system.
Insert notices in all Board of Public Utilities (BOPU) statements and
Cheyenne Light, Fuel & Power (CLF&P) bills;
•
Promote a 311-style approach, using downtown as the test case
before rolling out the “Click a Concern” system City-wide;
•
Hold public walkabouts; invite interested citizens to join officials in a
tour of favorite irritants – dirty tree grates, blocked drains, full trash
bins, etc.  Officials could arrange for repair on-the-spot or contact
the appropriate party for attention later on… all the while educating
the public about who does what.
Specific Tasks
Responsible
Parties
Time
Allowance
Success
Metric
Budget
Allowance
1  Compile a comprehensive list of who does what in
downtown’s public realm.  Study the current system for
overlaps, gaps or unclear, unclaimed responsibilities and
clues for more efficient operations.  Design and print an
attractive leaflet giving contact numbers for sidewalks,
curbs, street signs, trash bins, etc.  Distribute the leaflet
touting the “Request Partner” system via BOPU and CLF&P
bills within two months.  Insert reminder leaflets several
times each year.
2  Allow six months for downtown Cheyenne’s 311
system to be up-and-running.  (Over 100 cities offer non-
emergency 311 service, from San Francisco to Shertz,
Texas.  One place installed their 311 system in eleven
minutes.)
3  Schedule public walkabouts each spring; note every
plus and minus in downtown’s public realm.  With
clipboards, cameras and cell phones in-hand, elected
officials will guide the tours with agency heads, property
owners, tenants and other downtown stakeholders to list
every item in need of attention – lamp posts, bike racks,
benches, banners, historic plaques, parking signs, etc.
Three-months’ planning is needed.  Answer all complaints
at least two weeks before CFD 2013.
