### Page 52 of 84

```text
City Strategy 23
Convene a Downtown Planning Summit for
Today’s Stakeholders
Project Leader:     City
Logical Partners:  DDA
Need
Widespread community participation is a touchstone in Cheyenne:
witness Vision 2020; Progress and Prosperity, and; Plan Cheyenne…
Community leaders and involved citizens tackle issues and create
notable achievements.  It’s time to deploy that energy and experience to
benefit downtown:  the home base for the State, Laramie County, all City
agencies, the library, Civic Center, the Cheyenne Regional Medical Center
and the economic development triad of Visit Cheyenne, Cheyenne
LEADS, and the Chamber.  Over 50 statewide professional groups
maintain offices near the Capitol.  Employees supporting these activities
comprise about half of the 16,400 office workers in downtown
Cheyenne.  Downtown also hosts major civic events:  arts and crafts
shows; heritage fairs; parades; and festivals like Cheyenne Frontier
Days…
Over time, coordination improves as participants share information, plan
together, and explore partnerships that collectively conserve public
funds.  Right now parallel efforts are underway to:
•
attract job candidates and, at the same time, provide superior
housing options leading to shorter employee commutes;
•
encourage more growth downtown, e.g., by using brownfields
mitigation to make more land available;
•
leverage the proposed public safety building;
•
investigate joint-use utility cores to help attract development and
encourage historic property rehabilitation.
But each action by existing stakeholders appears to be happening in a
solitary silo… contrary to Cheyenne tradition.
Remedy
The City should convene a formal, public downtown planning summit.
Initial topics will include such issues affecting downtown Cheyenne as:
land assembly; new construction; retrofit of historic fabric; making wise
choices the path of least regulatory resistance.  Discussions will likely
yield a number of what-ifs, answered only by inviting more people to a
subsequent summit geared toward coordination and strategy
refinement.
Notes
Home-grown, whole-community discussion is the tradition in Cheyenne.
Revive it and focus on downtown.
