City Strategy 24
Implement the Next Steps in PlanCheyenne
Project Leader:     City
Logical Partners:  MPO, SHPO and CHPB
Need
The Chamber spearheaded Vision 2020 in 2001.  Five years later, the
Market-Based Downtown Plan outlined the economics of downtown
revitalization.  Recently, PlanCheyenne identified downtown as a major
focus.  Cheyenne’s newly adopted Unified Development Code (UDC) sets
design standards for some areas of the City.  Now is the time to combine
all of these ideas and tackle a comprehensive approach to downtown.
Remedy
Begin with three new sets of design and building guidelines:
• First, guidelines for new construction within the CBD.  This district
offers the greatest flexibility of land use and the highest density in
the City.
• Second, guidelines for new construction within downtown’s historic
district calling for sensitivity to the context of older buildings’ height,
design details and materials.  Some of these new guidelines might be
translated from DDA’s existing guidelines.
•
Third, design and building guidelines for certified historic
rehabilitation and retrofit of buildings listed as part of the downtown
historic district.  These structures must have their own set of
reasonable and easily achievable design guidelines and building
codes, again using DDA’s guidelines as a template.  This third set will
also use the Secretary of the Interior’s Standards and the new
Guidelines on Sustainability.
Notes
There will always be philosophical differences on design and building
guidelines – development standards.  However, to encourage investment
of millions in private dollars, Cheyenne needs to create an environment
that provides certainty to investors.  Few cities have the opportunity to
create something entirely new in design and building guidelines.  It’s
appropriate that the award-winning PlanCheyenne will prompt other
awards – rational, easy-to-use and completely sustainable guidelines for
rehab, new construction and infill.

