City Strategy 25
Plan for Major Downtown Infill
Development
Project Leader      City
Logical Partners   MPO, DDA and the public
Need
Avoiding sprawl and its infrastructure costs and
reviving downtown while accommodating population
growth entails thoughtful reuse of buildings and
under-utilized land.  At least 100,000 SF of historic
fabric remains vacant, ready for rehabilitation,
retrofit and reuse.  Within 63 City blocks – from 15th
to 24th and Capitol to Snyder – 35 acres are vacant
(not including streets).  Twelve acres – 35 percent –
are owned by the public; 65 percent is held by only
42 individuals.  Cheyenne can leverage these assets
to create a cooperative plan for rehab, reuse and new
construction without displacing any businesses
homes or churches:   a new town, downtown.
Remedy
Letting real estate market forces play out retards infill
and redevelopment.  The most effective intervention
is to forge a private/public partnership to:
• Begin with “Drawings, Designs and Dreams”
(Strategy 7).  Incorporate concepts from
Strategies 11 through 14.
• Consider the benefits of a community land trust
(Strategy 33).  Use the historic rehab investment
consortium and the proposed “synthetic” tax
credit (Strategies 35 through 37).
• Blend new construction and historic rehab. Create
vistas from the near west side to the Capitol and
Depot Plaza.  Create a signature skyline.
• Design new vehicular access from Missile Drive
into downtown, easing congestion on Lincolnway.
Add bikeways and greenways between MLK Park
and Holliday Park.  Plan a downtown “Central
Park” with the old steam plant as centerpiece.
Use Crow Creek as an amenity.  Add recreation
and healthy activities.
Provide more housing options.  Add affordable office
and retail space.  Find places for the grocery store
(Strategy 9), the “Idea Lab” (Strategy 11), the artists’
co-op (Strategy 38) and high-tech and energy
companies that provide employment opportunities.
