```text
City Strategy 26
Revisit Downtown’s Parking Fee
Structure
Project Leader:     City
Logical Partners:  DDA/DCMA, CTP, Visit Cheyenne, the Chamber
Need
Many Placemaking interviews lasted exactly two
hours due to downtown’s parking regulations.  Free
parking is a nice amenity, but it engenders anxiety
about incurring a $10 ticket for exceeding the two-
hour limit.  The garage also angst-ridden.  Parking in
downtown Cheyenne calls for strategic management.
To provide better management, improve parking
turnover and generate more revenue, many cities opt
for pay-station technology.  Solar-powered, electronic
meters accept coins, tokens, credit cards, etc.
Usually, one pay-station serves up to twelve spaces.
A customer pays; the pay-station prints a receipt; the
receipt is displayed on the dashboard for easy
monitoring.  It shows time-in, amount of time
purchased and expected time-out.  Pay-station
technology allows management to program varying
rates – the most desirable places at certain times,
hour-of-the-day, season… even free.  Most cities
recoup the cost of pay-stations within three years.
Remedy
The City should test pay-stations (perhaps in the
eight-blocks (76 block faces) bounded by Thomes,
Lincolnway, Central and 18th ) expanding them to all
lots and garages if they prove advantageous, and:
• Issue monthly (or annual) parking tags at reduced
rates for use on-street or in City lots/garages and
monitor their use to discourage violations;
• Through DCMA, offer pay-station tokens to
merchants so they can reward frequent shoppers;
• Remove parking signs to improve  curb appeal.
Charging a modest fee of 25¢ per hour would
generate over $200,000 and pay for itself in one year.
Notes
Some up-scale suburban shopping malls are installing
pay-station technology.  To combat meter “feeding,”
fees could increase with each time unit purchased.
Specific Tasks
Responsible
Parties
Time Allowance
Success
Metric
Budget
Allowance
