City Strategy 31
Revive S.R. DeBoer’s 1929 Street
Tree Program
Project Leader:     City
Logical Partners:  DDA and volunteers
Need
Cheyenne’s latitude, altitude and topography offer
landscaping challenges – constant wind, temperature
extremes, the semi-arid nature of the High Plains, and
rapid cool downs.  Placemaking participants
bemoaned hail damage to downtown’s 400 planters
in 2011 and commended property owners that
landscaped public areas along with downtown Ft.
Collins’ dense tree canopy.
S.R. DeBoer was a Denver-based landscape architect
who designed public parks, tree-lined boulevards and
private gardens in the western US.  At least 14 of his
projects are listed on the National Register of Historic
Places.  While designing and implementing
Cheyenne’s Holliday Park and Lake Minnehaha in the
late-1920s, there are hints that he became intrigued
with a tree-lined “power corridor” on Capitol Avenue
between the Wyoming State Capitol (the public
sector) on 24th and the Union Pacific Depot (the
private sector) on 15th.  By the early 1930s, DeBoer
and colleagues are said to have developed a street
tree plan for the city.
Remedy
It’s time to revisit and adapt DeBoer’s ideas – starting
downtown.  Arborists caution against DeBoer’s plans
for dense plantings of Russian Olive (now banned in
Wyoming) and Plains Cottonwood (the official state
tree).  Instead, professionals suggest colorful and
fast-growing trees such as Thornless Honeylocust,
Hackberry, Deborah Norway Maple, Burr Oak,
Littleleaf Linden, Box Elder and other trees favored by
sustainability advocates and permaculturalists.
Notes
An extensive, healthy urban forest offers numerous
benefits.  More street trees will increase downtown’s
beauty, boost property values, lower temperatures,
slow storm water runoff, improve water quality and
maintain air quality.  The USDA’s iTree and
OpenTreeMap software enable officials and residents
to register trees and monitor the impacts.
