City Strategy 32

Petition to Ease Wyoming’s Restaurant Liquor
License Laws for Cheyenne and Beyond
Project Leader:     City
Logical Partners:  DDA, Main Street, Visit
Cheyenne, WCCA and WAM
Need
The prospect of opening a restaurant gleams in many entrepreneurs’
eyes.  There’s a myth that 90 percent of restaurants fail in their initial
year.  In fact, it’s only 27 percent, in line with other businesses.  Almost
125 restaurants open nationwide every day; over 42,000 restaurant
licenses were issued in 2010.  What would make it easier for aspiring
restauranteurs in downtown Cheyenne?  Wyoming Statute §12-4-102(d)
mandates a population formula to determine the number of “retail,”
“restaurant” and “bar and grill” licenses.  In Cheyenne, there are no
“retail” licenses available (for on-site, off-site and package sales).  There
are unlimited “restaurant” licenses (no bar; table service only; $1,050
annually).  But there are only three unfilled “bar and grill” permits
(allowing a stand-up bar; $10,500 initially; $1,500 renewals).  Basic fees
are modest, but the limited number of “bar and grill” permits stymies.
Locally-owned restaurants are a hallmark of reviving downtowns.  The
locavore movement increases interest and demand.  Cheyenne’s urban
core is home to several exceptional dining options.  There should be
more restaurants offering enhanced choices, sparking greater interest
downtown, attracting more day- and night-time activity and opening
new eyes to downtown’s potential.
Remedy
The City and DDA should adopt legislative agendas to amend liquor
license laws .  The Wyoming Association of Municipalities, Wyoming
County Commissioners Association and Wyoming Main Street could help
in amending Statute §12-4-102(d).
• Exempt Wyoming Main Street’s 14 communities and affiliates from
the population formula.  (Almost 25 percent of Wyoming’s
population lives in these communities.  As Cheyenne and Casper join
Wyoming Main Street, that number jumps to 45 percent.)
• Exempt Wyoming’s two Metropolitan Statistical Areas (Casper and
Cheyenne) and the state’s seven Micropolitan Statistical Areas
(Evanston, Gillette, Jackson, Laramie, Riverton, Rock Springs and
Sheridan) from the population formula.  (About 73 percent of
Wyoming’s citizens live in these nine communities.)
Notes
As 17th Street’s Restaurant Row demonstrates, restaurants attract more
restaurants, more people and other businesses.
