```text
City Strategy 33
Organize a Downtown Community Land Trust
Project Leader:      City
Logical Partners:   MPO and DDA
Need
Community input was at the core of completing Vision 2020 and the
Market-Based Downtown Plan.  As well, PlanCheyenne successfully used
public engagement tools.  All three provide great ideas for downtown’s
future.  Meanwhile:
• The US Environmental Protection Agency’s $1 million Brownfields
Assessment Grant for Cheyenne will be implemented throughout and
adjacent to downtown – with stakeholder involvement.
• The MPO produced visions for downtowns near west side.
• The City is pursuing the Capitol Basin stormwater project on
Cheyenne’s front porch.
These public programs could leverage $200 to $400 million in private
investment in downtown Cheyenne.  Will the public continue to be
involved?  Is there a private/public partnership offering guidance?  Will
market forces prevail?  A community land trust (CLT) may provide a good
common-ground answer.
CLTs acquire and manage land for CLT members – primarily to preserve
affordability.  Think of CLT as a nonprofit condominium:  CLT owns and
manages the land; private interests or the public lease the land – usually
for 99 years – and own what’s built upon it.  CLT members can be any
entity:  developers; churches; home owners; businesses, etc.  But all
members have a say in what takes place – new development, historic
rehab, etc.  In effect, land value is taken out of the equation.  Value
comes from what is built on CLT’s property.  The tax base grows and
strengthens the local economy.
Remedy
In planning for major infill development (Strategy 25), a CLT can deliver
land at a lower cost, enabling development of more affordable and
higher value uses as determined by its members.  One place to begin
might be the 35 undeveloped acres on the near west side of which
twelve acres – 35 percent – is in public ownership.  Private owners of
adjacent lands could join the CLT too.
Notes
There are over 250 CLTs in 31 states including one chartered in
Wyoming: the Jackson Hole Community Housing Trust.  Cheyenne should
join the National Community Land Trust Network and develop sensible
site-specific approaches for downtown Cheyenne.
Specific Tasks
Responsible
Parties
Time
Allowance
Success
Metric
Budget
Allowance
