HPB Strategy34
Introduce a Tax Credit for Historic Rehabilitation
Project Leader:     CHPB
Logical Partners:  SHPO, DDA and State
Need
Most real estate investors pay federal income tax.  They can earn a 20
percent federal tax credit for eligible costs incurred in certified historic
rehabilitation of income-producing structures.  In addition, 30 states offer
additional benefits ranging from a five percent to 20 percent tax credit.  For
example, investors in Utah and North Carolina are entitled to a tax credit
against their federal income tax liability and another 20 percent against
their state income tax liability.
Of course, Wyoming has no state income tax.  No tax; no tax credit which
means no statewide incentive to save historic structures.  Consequently, it’s
no surprise that Wyoming lags other states in the volume of certified
historic rehab projects.
Remedy
A “synthetic” tax credit needs to be championed by project leaders, working
with Wyoming’s Business Council, which would create a financial buffer
between the (sometimes) higher costs of historic rehab and lower rates for
new construction.  Project sponsors would be eligible for state grants – tax
credits – to make up that difference.  A few of the benefits:
•
Creates jobs with firms doing business in Cheyenne;
•
Adds to the property tax base, increasing local revenue;
•
Builds a market for older buildings, thereby arresting a drop in
downtown’s real estate values;
•
Increases investments in downtown space – lowering some rents – but
opening new markets for more retail, office and residents;
•
Spawn retail activity via more restaurants and services – more jobs;
create a ripple effect on nearby streets.
To encourage a quick response, the synthetic tax credit might sunset in five
years.  It could also be retroactive for recent/current renovations.
Notes
• Maryland returns $8.53 for each $1.00 in state tax credits.  In Rhode
Island, it’s $5.35.  In Missouri, it’s $4.00… with 6,871 new jobs and
$60 million in new local revenue over three years.
• In ten years, Virginia’s state tax credit created an additional 10,700
jobs and $444 million in wages.
• Massachusetts’ tax credit leveraged investment of $50 million.
Nearby Colorado, Montana and Utah use state tax credits to encourage
rehab.  Why not Wyoming?
