### Page 65 of 84

```text
HPB Strategy35
Form a Historic Rehab Investment Consortium
Project Leader:      CHPB
Logical Partners:   SHPO, City, Private Investors
Need
The federal investment tax credit for rehabilitation of historic buildings
has a significant record:  two million jobs; investment of over $30 billion;
rehabilitation of 37,000 structures.  Under this program, investors
receive a 20 percent federal income tax credit for successful rehab.  It‘s
the kingpin in almost all downtown revitalization programs.
Indeed, the tax credit has been used in some of downtown Cheyenne’s
most prominent rehab projects.  The process is really quite simple.  It can
be tweaked to benefit a number of individuals and organizations – most
notably, owners of vacant or almost-vacant buildings, private investors
and nonprofits dedicated to preserving architectural heritage.
The National Trust for Historic Preservation publishes a list of the “11
most endangered buildings.” Preservationists in St. Louis and Dallas
maintain local lists.  Cheyenne has done the same but needs to broadly
publicize the targets – and take the next steps as well – to help save
downtown’s architectural jewels.
Remedy
The Cheyenne Historic Preservation Board (CHPB), along with the State
Historic Preservation Office (SHPO), should reorganize the existing
program and jointly name downtown’s eleven most endangered
buildings.  Criteria might include demolition threats, architectural
quality, role in Cheyenne’s history and/or street front prominence.  Once
the first buildings (and, perhaps, the second set too) have been selected,
CHPB should help form an investment consortium to acquire the
structure(s) in one of at least three ways:
• Negotiate to buy the property for something near appraised value;
• Offer the owner an equity position in the consortium equal to the
building’s appraised value;
• If the owner is a ground floor merchant, suggest an equity position
with the remaining value as a street level retail condominium.
The consortium would proceed with rehab and place an enhanced-value
structure on the tax roles.  With some changes to the Historic
Preservation Board’s charter, beneficiaries could include CHPB, current
owners, local investors, banks, etc.
Notes
This is an (almost) home-grown jump-start for historic rehabilitation of
some of downtown’s favorite structures.
