HPB Strategy36
Seed a Revolving Loan Fund for Historic Rehab
Project Leader:     CHPB
Logical Partners:  Main Street, WBC and DDA
Need
Many states maintain revolving loan programs for historic preservation.
Cities – large and small – have established programs to offer low-interest
loans for acquisition/restoration of historic structures to make up the
difference between the (sometimes) higher costs of historic rehab and
lower rates for new construction.  Loans may be available on a
competitive basis or targeted to public-interest groups working in under-
served communities.  Some loans may be made to preservation
nonprofits only.  Maximum loan amounts vary widely.  Most interest
rates are from two to four percent.
Remedy
It’s reasonable to propose a historic preservation revolving loan program
for downtown Cheyenne if it serves as a test case for statewide
application – perhaps in all Wyoming Main Street communities.  CHPB
(or a chartered subsidiary – perhaps with partners) should start such a
fund.  Seed monies might come from the City, Wyoming Business
Council, a legislative grant, USDA’s rural development program,
foundations and private donors – and demolition permit fees directed
toward preservation.  Or all of the above.  Begin CHPB’s discussions with
Wyoming Main Street and Wyoming Business Council.  Really, this is only
an extension of WBC’s Historic Architecture Assistance Fund.
• Enable private or nonprofit groups to apply for loans to acquire and
restore an historic downtown structure.
• Require that applicants complete exterior and interior rehabilitation
– to the Secretary of the Interior’s standards – and meeting all local
design and building regulations.
• Ask applicants to include street level retail and a mix of residential
and office on upper floors.
• Customize the program for the “Expanded Downtown Historic
District.”  Take a lien on a building.  Set a maximum loan amount of
15 percent of the revolving loan fund balance.  Adopt a five-year
repayment schedule.
• Devote landfill fees on all demolition permits to the revolving loan
fund.
Notes
Dozens of states and cities operate thriving, permanent, self-sufficient
revolving loan funds for preservation projects.  Cheyenne could too.
