Need
The Greater Cheyenne Chamber of Commerce offers two affiliate
programs to nurture future Laramie County leaders:  Leadership
Cheyenne (LC) and Cheyenne Young Professionals (CYP).
• Leadership Cheyenne enrolls an annual class of about 20 for a
rigorous, nine-month immersion in all-things Cheyenne.  LC is open
to all interested in civic leadership but applicants usually exceed the
target class size.  The main social event is the Boo Ball, an annual
Halloween fundraiser benefiting a selected charitable activity.  LC
costs about $700 per enrollee.
• Cheyenne Young Professionals is a networking opportunity for
younger adults.  Monthly luncheons offer speakers on four core
missions, one of which is volunteerism.  Several hundred members
are on the roster and annual dues are $40.
In a community that takes pride in home-grown initiatives, it’s
worthwhile to suggest beneficial links between LC and CYP as well as
between them and downtown Cheyenne – the employment site for most
members.
Remedy
There appears to be little interaction between these two initiatives
despite their common goals:  know Cheyenne; promote Cheyenne; lead
Cheyenne.  A focused joint program.
• Give the program a new name – Cheyenne Affiliates?  Don’t muddle
distinct functions of each, but leverage the combined energy.
• Plan joint sessions specifically on downtown revitalization – why
downtown is important to Cheyenne and Wyoming.
• Spotlight one existing downtown business each month.  Act as a
welcome wagon for new businesses and new residents.
• Stage four joint fundraising events each year – and keep the Boo Ball
too.  Select a project focus, identify sponsors, charge an entry fee,
invite the public and offer food and drink.  Donate proceeds
appropriately given the year’s focus.
Notes
Many Chambers are implementing joint programming for their younger
members.  One community Cheyenne’s size raises about $12,500 per
event… four times a year.
